    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1129">

                <pb facs="1045" n="787" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg787"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/2382</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-29T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 29, 1915, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 30, 2.45 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2511. An advertisement of high-explosive shells containing acids, said to have
                    been inserted in <hi rendition="#italic">American Machinist</hi> for May 6 last
                    by Cleveland Automatic Machine Company of Cleveland, Ohio, has been widely
                    reprinted in Germany and is causing unfortunate impression here particularly
                    words “fragments become coated with these acids in exploding and wounds caused
                    by them mean death in terrible agony within four hours if not attended to
                    immediately.”</p>
                <p>I should be glad to learn status of <hi rendition="#italic">American
                        Machinist</hi> and whether advertisement is authentic. It seems possible
                    that this is the work of German propagandists in the United States and if so,
                    prompt exposure would be helpful here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
